PLOT 2 OVERHAUL PATCH NOTE (vNext) — “Fix the proxy space” + Waqas-aligned small-world focus
===========================================================================================

Context / Why this patch exists
-------------------------------
Recent Plot 2 runs show weak or null separation between:
  - A (true random WS-Flex), B (proxy-filtered WS-Flex), and C (TPE-guided WS-Flex),
under both AUPC(alpha) and max_drop for ar1_drift, despite correct perturbation fingerprints
and regime-stratified selection.

Diagnostics indicate the core failure mode:
  (1) ORC is strongly correlated with k / density (|corr(ORC, k)| ≈ 0.95 in our pools).
  (2) Therefore, “maximize TE and ORC” behaves like “pick higher k” (density-dominated proxy space),
      collapsing Pareto structure and undermining the central claim (“invert Waqas et al.”).
  (3) Waqas et al. explore WS-Flex primarily in the (C, L) space (clustering coefficient and
      average shortest path length), which is a natural parameterization of small-world structure.
      Our current search does not constrain or optimize (C, L) — we only log them.

Goal of this patch:
  - Make “small-world regime” first-class (Waqas-aligned).
  - Redefine the training-free objectives so they are not redundant with k/density.
  - Preserve the A/B/C baseline logic needed to claim we “invert Waqas et al.”:
      A = truly random baseline (no proxy),
      B = proxy-selected baseline (static selection),
      C = adaptive search (TPE) using the same proxy.
  - Add training-free GO/NO-GO gates that must pass before any 24h training run.

Summary of key changes
----------------------
UPDATES / TWEAKS (existing behaviors changed)
  U1) Replace TE/ORC-only proxy with a Waqas-aligned proxy: (TE, small-worldness σ) as objectives.
  U2) Demote ORC to a tie-breaker or residual proxy (within-k residual), not a primary objective.
  U3) Make (C, L) binning mandatory for selection diversity across A/B/C, not optional logging.
  U4) Expand WS-Flex generator bounds to cover a wider (C, L) manifold (not just k/p grids),
      while keeping explicit capacity control (H fixed; optional edge-budget bands).

NEW MECHANISMS (new functions / stages)
  N1) Small-worldness computation σ using ER-matched references (C_ER, L_ER).
  N2) Proxy viability diagnostic + strict GO/NO-GO gating based on redundancy and Pareto width.
  N3) Residualized curvature proxy ORC_res = ORC − E[ORC | k] (or rank-within-k) for tie-breaking.
  N4) Optional normalized spectral radius proxy ρ_norm for stability control (constraint or tie-breaker),
      with density-aware normalization to avoid reintroducing “proxy = k”.
  N5) Small-world focused generator mode that samples parameters to hit target (C, L) bins
      (WS-Flex-in-(C,L) sampling), consistent with the WS-Flex “design space” framing.

Definitions / Equations (locked)
--------------------------------
Graph and notation:
  - Let G = (V, E), with |V| = H (hidden units fixed, default H=32).
  - Let A be the adjacency matrix of the directed wiring actually used for the CfC mask.

(1) Degree-distribution entropy (TE proxy)
  - Let p_d be the empirical degree distribution over nodes (use undirected degree or total degree,
    but be consistent across all candidates; default: undirected degree for WS-Flex before orientation).
  - Shannon entropy of degrees:
      H_deg(G) = − Σ_d p_d log p_d
  - Normalized topological entropy (TE):
      TE(G) = H_deg(G) / log(H)          ∈ [0, 1]   (define TE=0 if distribution degenerate)
  Justification:
    TE captures degree heterogeneity / mixing and was used as a robustness-correlated proxy in prior work.
    Unlike ORC, TE is not purely monotone in k if the generator induces degree variability.

(2) Clustering coefficient C and average path length L
  - Use standard definitions on the undirected WS-Flex graph (pre-orientation):
      C(G) = average local clustering coefficient
      L(G) = average shortest path length (only for connected graphs)
  Justification:
    In WS-type families, (C, L) provides a smooth map of the “small-world manifold” and is the primary
    coordinate system used in Waqas-style WS-Flex explorations. TE/ORC alone does not control C/L.

(3) ER reference values for matched density
  - Let p_edge be the observed edge probability (undirected):
      p_edge = 2|E| / (H(H−1))
  - Define an ER reference graph G_ER ~ G(H, p_edge) (or analytic approximations).
  - Compute reference clustering and path length (choose one method and lock it):
      C_ER ≈ p_edge
      L_ER ≈ log(H) / log(H·p_edge)       (valid when H·p_edge > 1)
    (If H·p_edge ≤ 1 or approximation unstable, compute L_ER via Monte Carlo averaging over
     R_ER replicates, default R_ER=20, with fixed seed schedule.)

(4) Small-worldness score σ (single scalar)
      σ(G) = (C(G)/C_ER) / (L(G)/L_ER)
  Justification:
    σ increases with “high clustering + short paths” relative to random graphs at matched density,
    matching the intuitive and commonly used operationalization of “small-world”.

(5) Ollivier–Ricci curvature (ORC), sign-preserving
  - Compute mean Ollivier–Ricci curvature over edges of the undirected graph:
      ORC(G) = (1/|E|) Σ_(u,v)∈E κ(u,v)      (no absolute value)
  Justification:
    We retain sign information. However, ORC is density-coupled in our WS-Flex space and thus is
    not suitable as a primary objective without residualization.

(6) Residualized ORC within k (tie-breaker proxy)
  - Estimate mean ORC for each k in a reference set:
      μ_ORC(k) = E[ ORC(G) | k ]
  - Define residual curvature:
      ORC_res(G) = ORC(G) − μ_ORC(k(G))
  Justification:
    Removes the dominant dependence on k/density while preserving curvature variability within regime.
    Using ORC_res prevents the optimizer from “winning” by simply choosing larger k.

(7) Spectral radius (optional stability proxy), density-aware
  - Let ρ(A) be the spectral radius of the *directed* adjacency used for masking:
      ρ(A) = max_i |λ_i(A)|
  - Normalize to reduce degree coupling:
      ρ_norm(A) = ρ(A) / max(1, \bar d)
    where \bar d is the mean out-degree (or mean total degree) of the directed wiring.
  Justification:
    Raw ρ(A) often scales with degree. ρ_norm is a “shape/stability” proxy rather than a density proxy.

Overhauled Proxy Space (locked)
-------------------------------
Primary objectives (used for Pareto/TPE):
  Objective 1 (maximize): TE(G)
  Objective 2 (maximize): σ(G)      [small-worldness]

Secondary proxies (NOT used as main objectives):
  Tie-breaker 1: ORC_res(G)         (prefer higher ORC_res)
  Tie-breaker 2 (optional): ρ_norm(A) constraint band or tie-breaker
    - Default as constraint: ρ_norm ∈ [ρ_min, ρ_max] (per k-regime; see below)

Why this is the correct fix:
  - It makes small-world structure explicit (Waqas-aligned).
  - It removes the “ORC ≈ k” collapse from the primary proxy space.
  - It preserves interpretability: TE and σ have clear structural meanings.

Overhauled Plot 2 Baselines (A/B/C) — reviewer-proof inversion of Waqas
----------------------------------------------------------------------
A (True Random Baseline):
  - Sample graphs uniformly from the same WS-Flex generator constraints
    (connectedness + capacity constraints + (C,L) bin coverage), WITHOUT using any proxy ranking.
  - Selection within bins is uniform random.

B (Static Proxy Baseline):
  - Use the SAME candidate pool as A.
  - Select graphs using the proxy score derived from (TE, σ) (Pareto/crowding within bins).
  - No adaptive search; just “training-free filtering”.

C (Adaptive Proxy NAS):
  - Use Optuna TPE multi-objective search over generator parameters to adaptively sample candidates
    that improve the Pareto front in (TE, σ).
  - Then select B graphs from the resulting candidate set using the same bin-aware selection policy.

Justification:
  - A vs B isolates “does the proxy space predict robustness at all?”
  - B vs C isolates “does adaptivity (TPE) beat static filtering under the same proxy?”
  - This structure avoids the earlier pitfall “Best TE+ORC vs Best TE+ORC”.

Generator / Search Space Overhaul (WS-Flex only; no new graph families)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Current generator parameters:
  - k ∈ even integers within [2, H−2] (via regimes)
  - p ∈ [0, 1]
  - connectedness constraint only

New generator modes (WS-Flex still; richer manifold coverage):
  Mode G0 (legacy): (k,p) sampling (kept for backward compatibility)
  Mode G1 (small-world targeted): sample to cover bins in (C,L) (or σ) explicitly

G1 Implementation concept:
  - Maintain the k-regimes (super_sparse, sparse, moderate, near_dense) to ensure capacity stratification.
  - Within each regime, define fixed bin edges for clustering and path length:
      C bins: {low, mid, high}
      L bins: {low, mid, high}
    where bin edges are computed ONCE from a large reference set (see N2) and frozen in the manifest.
  - Candidate generation proceeds by attempting to fill each (regime, Cbin, Lbin) cell with quota q.

Justification:
  - Forces exploration of the WS-Flex manifold the way Waqas explored it (explicit C/L space).
  - Prevents collapse to “near_dense only” Pareto points.
  - Gives us an honest, controllable “small-world focus”: we can define which bins correspond to “small-world”
    (high C, low L) and emphasize those without excluding others.

Capacity matching and fairness constraints (tightened)
-----------------------------------------------------
We lock capacity in the recurrent chamber, while allowing topology to vary.

Locked (must be identical across A/B/C and all WS-Flex graphs):
  - Hidden dimension H (default 32).
  - Same CNN feature extractor and same CfC cell hyperparameters.
  - Same training protocol, same seeds, same subjects, same eval mode.
  - Same directed-edge orientation policy and wiring_seed usage.

Constraints:
  - Graph must be connected (nx.is_connected).
  - Optional edge-budget bands within each k-regime remain allowed, but must be applied identically to A/B/C.
  - If ρ_norm constraint enabled: enforce per-regime band:
      ρ_norm ∈ [0.8, 1.2] default (tunable; see decision points)

External baselines (non-WS-Flex):
  - External Random (ncps Random) remains a separate “family” baseline.
  - It must be capacity-matched explicitly:
      - Same H for recurrent state
      - Same projection sizes
    If strict matching is not possible due to library wiring semantics, label it clearly as
    “different wiring family baseline” and do not include it in A/B/C proxy inversion claims.

Justification:
  - Keeps the causal claim “wiring topology alone” defensible for A/B/C.
  - Prevents misleading wins/losses due to hidden size or IO structure differences.

Selection policy overhaul (mandatory diversity; fixed bins)
----------------------------------------------------------
Replace dynamic per-method tertile bins with fixed, manifest-locked bins.

New selection coverage level:
  coverage_level = "regime_cl_bins_fixed"

Bin edges:
  - Derived from a reference set of size M_ref (default 5000 connected graphs) using G1 or G0 sampling.
  - For each regime r:
      - Compute quantiles for C and L across all candidates in r:
          C edges: q = (1/3, 2/3)
          L edges: q = (1/3, 2/3)
      - Freeze these edges in manifest as C_edges[r], L_edges[r].

Selection within bins:
  - A: uniform random within each (r, Cbin, Lbin) cell.
  - B: within each cell, rank by proxy (see below).
  - C: select from TPE candidate set using same cell quotas and ranking.

Proxy ranking (within-cell):
  - Multi-objective score via Pareto + crowding distance in (TE, σ).
  - Tie-breakers:
      1) ORC_res (descending)
      2) ρ_norm proximity to center of band (if constraint enabled)

Justification:
  - Avoids “selection changes because bin edges were recomputed per method/pool”.
  - Prevents A/B/C overlap collapse and maintains comparable structural diversity.
  - Tie-breakers add signal without letting ORC or ρ reintroduce density dominance.

Training-free search overhaul (Optuna TPE)
------------------------------------------
Objective function:
  Return (TE(G), σ(G)).

Candidate evaluation:
  - Generate graph using WS-Flex (G0 or G1 mode).
  - Reject if disconnected.
  - Compute TE, C, L, σ.
  - Compute ORC, then ORC_res using μ_ORC(k) from reference set.
  - Compute ρ_norm on the directed adjacency used for masking (optional).

Early stopping:
  - Keep hypervolume early stopping, but compute hypervolume on (TE, σ) Pareto front.
  - Stop when:
      (HV_t − HV_{t−W}) / max(ε0, HV_{t−W}) < eps for patience windows
    with defaults:
      W=5 batches, eps=0.01, patience=3, ε0=1e-6.
  - Additionally require Pareto-width criterion (N2) before allowing early stop to trigger.

Justification:
  - TE/σ front should be less degenerate than TE/ORC under density coupling.
  - Early stopping is still needed for compute discipline, but must not terminate prematurely in a collapsed proxy space.

NEW Stage: Proxy viability diagnostic + GO/NO-GO gates (must pass before training)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
N2: run_plot2_proxy_viability.py (new script)

Inputs:
  - H, k-regimes, generator mode (G0/G1), M_ref (default 5000)
Outputs:
  - reference_metrics.csv
  - proxy_viability_report.json (gate pass/fail + reasons)
  - frozen_bin_edges.json (C_edges, L_edges per regime)
  - μ_ORC(k) table for ORC_res

Gates (strict):
  GATE V1 (redundancy):
    For each primary objective proxy P ∈ {TE, σ}, require:
      |corr(P, k)| < 0.80  and  |corr(P, density)| < 0.80
    (We EXPECT ORC to fail; ORC is not a primary objective.)

  GATE V2 (Pareto width):
    Pareto front in (TE, σ) must contain >= 10 points AND
    include candidates from >= 3 of 4 k-regimes AND
    include >= 6 distinct (Cbin, Lbin) cells overall.

  GATE V3 (cell feasibility):
    For each regime r, at least 6 of the 9 (C,L) cells must be non-empty
    in the reference set (prevents impossible stratification).

  If any gate fails: DO NOT PROCEED TO TRAINING.
  Fix bounds / generator mode and rerun viability diagnostic.

Justification:
  - This is the “save 24 hours” mechanism. It ensures we are not optimizing a collapsed proxy space.

Intermediate Diagnostic Mini Run (fast, before full Plot 2)
----------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
  Confirm that changing topology along TE/σ actually changes robustness under the chosen perturbation,
  without committing to full-scale training.

Procedure:
  - Select 1 graph per k-regime × 2 cells:
      small-world cell: (C high, L low)
      non-small-world cell: (C low, L high)
    Total = 4 regimes × 2 = 8 graphs.
  - Train/eval with:
      subjects=3, seeds=1, same ar1_drift settings, same alpha grid.

Success criteria (GO/NO-GO):
  - Primary: max_pairwise_delta_max_drop ≥ 0.03 across the 8 graphs
  - Secondary: at least one regime shows small-world cell more robust than non-small-world cell
              (directional evidence topology matters)
  If fail:
    - Increase perturbation severity OR change perturbation type selection (see below) before full-scale run.

Justification:
  - Directly tests whether (C,L)/σ variation is visible under current perturbation.
  - Avoids another 24h run if topology is genuinely second-order under this stress test.

Perturbation diagnosis (avoid wasting training runs)
----------------------------------------------------
We must ensure we are using a perturbation that exposes topology differences, not one dominated by CfC dynamics.

Add a training-free (or 1-model) perturbation sensitivity sweep stage:
  - Choose a single fixed WS-Flex graph (moderate regime) and sweep perturbation types:
      {ar1_drift, spatial_gaussian, emg_band, temporal_jitter} with matched SNR grid.
  - Compute max_drop and AUPC for each perturbation.
  - Pick the perturbation where:
      (i) max_drop is large enough (e.g., ≥ 0.15 at max intensity),
      (ii) curves are non-saturated (not flat),
      (iii) perturbation fingerprint passes.

This stage may reuse existing pilot scripts; if absent, implement:
  - run_plot2_perturbation_sensitivity.py (new).

Justification:
  - If all graphs look robust, we can’t prove anything about topology.
  - Choose the “most damaging but realistic” perturbation before full training.

Locked reporting / analysis updates (to match new design)
--------------------------------------------------------
Analysis remains largely correct; update only what is needed:

  - Group definitions:
      A = baseline_a (true random)
      B = baseline_b (proxy-filtered)
      C = tpe (adaptive)
  - Primary metric remains max_drop (lower is better).
  - Secondary: AUPC(alpha) remains reported, but not used as the sole success signal.
  - Add reporting of σ, (C,L) bins, and ORC_res stats per method.

Success criteria for full Plot 2 (mini then full)
-------------------------------------------------
Mini-scale (subjects=3, seeds=1):
  - Pass proxy viability gates V1–V3.
  - Pass intermediate diagnostic:
      max_pairwise_delta_max_drop ≥ 0.03 and at least one regime directional.

Full-scale:
  - A vs B (proxy validity): mean(max_drop_B − max_drop_A) < 0 with CI excluding 0 OR
    ≥ 2/3 subjects show B better than A (directional) AND effect size |d| ≥ 0.4.
  - B vs C (adaptive benefit): mean(max_drop_C − max_drop_B) < 0 with CI excluding 0 OR
    ≥ 2/3 subjects show C better than B AND effect size |d| ≥ 0.4.
  - Report AUPC as supporting, not decisive.

Decision points (defaults)
--------------------------
D1) Generator mode:
  Default: G1 (small-world targeted) for reference set + candidate pools.
  Rationale: ensures manifold coverage in (C,L), aligned with Waqas.

D2) Spectral radius usage:
  Default: OFF as objective, ON as optional constraint using ρ_norm.
  Rationale: avoid proxy redundancy; treat ρ_norm as stability guardrail.

D3) ORC usage:
  Default: tie-breaker only using ORC_res; never primary objective.
  Rationale: observed ORC–k coupling.

D4) Perturbation:
  Default: ar1_drift at target_snr_db=-6 with verified fingerprint.
  If intermediate diagnostic fails: escalate severity (e.g., -8 dB) or switch to more damaging
  perturbation found by sensitivity sweep.

